From 4b886a96e94260b5dcb6c5de2c77f3f9c6134949 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:18:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 06/19] feat(core): batch delete, scale and label by selector
 with per-item results (C8)

Bulk work cost one call per object: job 14 of the native session scaled
16 Deployments and deleted 15 ConfigMaps in 31 calls; job 5 relabelled
six objects with six full re-applies (6,994 tokens); job 10 deleted 12
objects one by one.

- resources_delete and resources_scale take `labelSelector` as an
  alternative to `name` (exactly one of the two). Every object of the
  kind in the namespace that matches is handled on its own, and the
  result has one line per object (deleted / deletion pending / scaled
  from -> to / failed with the API's reason). The header states the
  counts and the batch semantics: best_effort, a failure neither stops
  nor undoes the others. If any object failed, the call is an error.
- `dry_run: true` with a selector lists the objects that would be
  affected and changes nothing (validate first).
- An empty selector is refused, so a batch over a whole kind must be
  asked for by name. A batch delete sends each object's uid as a
  precondition, so an object recreated after the list is not deleted.
- New tool resources_label: set or remove (null) labels on one object
  or on every match of a selector, with a JSON merge patch on
  metadata.labels; other labels are left alone. Annotated destructive
  (a label change can move objects out of a Service's or ReplicaSet's
  selector) and idempotent.

RBAC metadata gains the list verb that a selector needs. `name` is no
longer required by the schema of resources_delete and resources_scale;
the handlers still refuse a call with neither, with the old message.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
